    text: "The drilled molars of Mehrgarh are the oldest dentistry known, but that practice had faded long before the cities. Harappan skeletons show what a diet of ground wheat and barley did: caries, worn crowns, abscesses and tooth loss were common, grit from the querns wearing teeth down as starch rotted them. What people did for the pain is not recorded. Later South Asian practice offers plausible guesses, chewing twigs to clean the teeth, pressing herbs or hot oil into a cavity, pulling a rotten tooth, but for the Harappans themselves it remains a guess.",
