    text: "Chola inscriptions record donors giving cows, buffaloes or ewes to a temple so that herders would supply ghee for a perpetual lamp, a set measure every day forever; the herder took the increase and the milk as his pay. Village cattle ploughed the paddy and grazed on commons and fallow; the herding communities, Idaiyar, moved with them. Beyond the plains lay the kurinji hill forests and scrub where people hunted deer and pig, cut timber and bamboo, collected honey and forest produce, and where hill peoples such as the Kurumbar and Maravar lived partly outside the village order.",
