    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The nagaram and paddy as money",
    text: "Each cluster of Tamil villages had a market town governed by its nagaram, an assembly of merchants who collected dues, kept the market and gave to the temple. Gold kasu coins were used for large sums, but much of daily exchange and nearly all wages and rents were in paddy, measured by the temple's standard marakkal. Oil, cloth, salt, betel and metalware were bought with paddy or coins. Merchant guilds such as the Manigramam and the Five Hundred ran caravans of pack-bullocks between towns and ports, with armed guards, and carts carried grain from the delta to the temples.",
