    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Uttaramerur's pot of names",
    text: "Tamil villages governed themselves through assemblies: the ur of ordinary villages and the sabha of Brahmin villages, often with a nadu council over a district. Inscriptions at Uttaramerur from about 920 CE set out how the sabha chose its committees by kudavolai, 'pot and ticket': names of qualified men were written on palm leaves, put in a pot, and a young boy drew them out before the assembly. Committees ran the tanks, gardens, justice and accounts. News and decisions were published by carving them on temple walls, so the temple was the village noticeboard, and a royal order was read aloud in the assembly.",
    wiki: ["Uthiramerur", "Chola dynasty"],
