    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kammalar, weavers and temple towns",
    text: "Tamil craftworkers clustered around temples and market towns. The kammalar, five artisan communities of goldsmiths, coppersmiths, blacksmiths, carpenters and stone masons, claimed descent from Vishvakarma and, under the Cholas, won rights such as blowing conches at their ceremonies. The Kaikolar weavers, also temple soldiers and servants, and the Saliyar produced cotton cloth for local use and export. Temples employed potters, garland makers, oil pressers and lamp-makers on fixed allowances of paddy. Crafts lived in their own streets, and the craft right was an inheritance.",
