    text: "Pallava and Chola kings created new settlements by grant: a brahmadeya gave a village's revenue to Brahmin families, who moved in, dug tanks and reorganised the fields, while existing cultivators became tenants. Families moving to a new place took up fields on lease from temples and landlords. In hard times households had few protections: Chola inscriptions record people in famine selling themselves and their descendants to a temple as servants in return for food, and others pledging land or cattle. Temples also lent grain and money at interest, and remitted dues when fields were ruined by flood or drought.",
