    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Letters in the sand on the thinnai",
    text: "In south Indian villages a teacher held school on the raised verandah of a house or temple, the thinnai, and children learned to write by tracing letters with a finger in sand spread on the floor, then graduating to scratching palm leaves with an iron stylus. They learned the alphabet, arithmetic tables by chanting, and moral verses such as the Tirukkural or Kannada vachanas. A child's first letters were written on an auspicious day with a ceremony, aksharabhyasam or vidyarambham. Brahmin boys went on to the Vedas at ghatikas and mathas. For most children the future lay in a hereditary occupation learned at home.",
