    topics: [{ workplace: "pasture" }, { workplace: "wild" }],
    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "Commons, jungle and the tiger in the grass",
    text: "Between the villages of medieval north India lay much uncultivated land: grazing commons, dhak and scrub jungle, the tall grass of the river margins and the Terai forest to the north. Village cattle and buffaloes were driven there daily by boys, and herding communities moved larger herds seasonally. The jungle was also a larder and a danger: people cut fuel and thatch, gathered mahua and wild fruit, and hunted deer and pig, while tigers and wolves took cattle. Kings and sultans hunted there for sport and to train armies, and the Sultans' campaigns against 'rebel' forest peoples cleared jungle by force.",
