    topics: [{ workplace: "extraction" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Zawar zinc and Sambhar salt",
    text: "At Zawar in Mewar, miners dug deep galleries for lead-zinc ore, and smelters there had mastered, by about the 9th century, the distillation of zinc in rows of clay retorts turned upside down over a furnace, the first large-scale zinc production anywhere. Nearby Khetri yielded copper. The salt lake at Sambhar was raked every dry season for salt that went across north India by cart and Banjara bullock. Stone for temples and mosques was quarried in the sandstone hills of Rajasthan and the Vindhyas. Mining was dangerous work for low-status labourers and rulers took the profits.",
    wiki: ["Zawar", "Sambhar Salt Lake", "Zinc"],
