    topics: [{ workplace: "market" }, { goal: "barter" }, { workplace: "carrying" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Hatta and mandapika: the medieval market town",
    text: "North Indian inscriptions of the 9th to 12th centuries name periodic markets (hatta) and market centres (mandapika) where local produce met traders' goods, with dues on each cartload, oil press and shop assigned to temples. Under the Delhi Sultans the silver tanka and copper jital, introduced by Iltutmish, spread coined money into villages as revenue came to be paid in cash. Grain moved on Banjara bullock trains and bullock carts; porters carried loads on shoulder poles and pilgrims walked. Village exchange was still often in kind, grain for oil, cloth, salt or a potter's pots, and bargaining was loud and expected.",
