    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Kotwal, qazi and muhtasib",
    text: "In the towns of the Delhi Sultanate order was kept by the kotwal, head of the city police and watch; the qazi judged by Islamic law, above all for Muslims, while Hindus largely kept their own caste and village courts; the muhtasib inspected markets, weights and public morals. Before the Sultans, Rajput and Gahadavala towns had their councils (panchakula) and royal officers. Heralds proclaimed orders in the bazaar with drums, and royal news-writers reported to the court. For ordinary people news came from travellers at the sarai, pilgrims to Kashi and Prayag, and talk at the well and market.",
