    topics: [{ workplace: "workshop" }, { occasion: "day.craft-power" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Spinning wheel, carding bow and the Sultan's karkhana",
    text: "The medieval north gained new craft tools: the spinning wheel (charkha) and the carding bow spread in the 13th and 14th centuries, probably from the Islamic world, speeding up cotton thread-making, and the treadle loom was refined. Muhammad bin Tughluq's karkhanas in Delhi employed thousands of silk weavers and embroiderers. In Varanasi and other towns weavers, metalworkers, stone carvers and dyers worked in family workshops along craft streets, many of them Muslim converts from weaving castes, the Julahas. Hindu artisans honoured Vishvakarma and their tools; Muslim artisans sought the blessing of Sufi saints, and many claimed a patron saint of their craft.",
    wiki: ["Spinning wheel", "Momin Ansari", "Muhammad bin Tughluq"],
