    title: "Chulha, grindstone and purity in the medieval kitchen",
    text: "In a north Indian house of the Sultanate age women's work began before dawn at the hand mill (chakki), grinding wheat or barley for the day's flatbreads, then cooking dal, vegetables and rice on a clay chulha fed with dung cakes. In higher-caste homes the kitchen was the purest room: no one entered in outdoor clothes, cooked food was not shared across caste lines, and a polluted pot was broken. Churning curd into butter and ghee, cleaning grain, spinning cotton, plastering floors with dung and caring for children filled the rest of the day. Ibn Battuta noted the khichri of rice and mung eaten by all.",
