    text: "When Muhammad bin Tughluq's heavy taxes and a drought brought famine to the Doab in the 1330s, peasants fled to the forests; the Sultan then lent money to cultivators to dig wells and buy seed and oxen, the loans called sondhar, an early form of the later taqavi. Firuz Shah Tughluq cut canals from the Yamuna and founded new towns such as Hisar. Households recovered by moving: new villages were settled on cleared jungle, often by one caste lineage, with the founder's descendants holding land rights. Others borrowed grain from the village moneylender and repaid at harvest with steep interest.",
