    text: "Vijayanagara's gold coin was the varaha, called the pagoda by Europeans, with half and quarter pieces and tiny gold fanams for smaller sums, and copper for everyday buying. Village weekly markets, santhe, sold grain, cloth, pots and livestock, and much small trade was still in kind. Goods moved on long trains of pack-bullocks run by Lambadi and other carrier communities, on carts along the roads to the ports, and on porters' heads. Paes marvelled at the city's bazaars piled with rice, pulses, fruit, rubies and pearls, and at merchants from Portugal, Persia and Arabia. Toll stations collected dues at every crossing.",
