    text: "Vijayanagara kings granted territories to nayakas, military chiefs who collected revenue and supplied troops, a system called nayankara. In each village a set of hereditary servants, the ayagars, ran things: the headman (gauda), the accountant (karnam), the watchman (talari), and the carpenter, smith, washerman, barber and others, all paid in grain shares and small plots. Every autumn the nayakas came to the capital for the Mahanavami festival to renew loyalty and present tribute, watched by foreign visitors from the royal platform. Temples were also civic centres, holding land, lending money and settling disputes.",
