    text: "Mughal revenue manuals such as the Ain-i Akbari list dozens of crops for each province and season: wheat, barley, gram and mustard in the rabi season after the monsoon; rice, millets, pulses, cotton and sugarcane in the kharif; and valuable crops, jins-i kamil, such as indigo, opium and tobacco, the last newly arrived from the Americas. The peasant, raiyat, ploughed with a pair of oxen, watered from wells and tanks, and paid a third to half the crop through the zamindar or the jagirdar's agent. Village cattle grazed the commons and fallows; herding castes kept buffaloes for ghee sold in the towns.",
