    text: "Mughal emperors hunted in the great ring-hunt, the qamargha, in which thousands of beaters drove game into a closing circle, and used cheetahs trained to course blackbuck; Jahangir recorded his kills in his memoir. Forests were still extensive and villagers hunted, trapped and gathered at the edges. Rivers were highways: boatmen carried grain, salt and stone on the Yamuna and Ganga, bridges of boats crossed them for armies, and at Delhi the Yamuna ran beside the Red Fort. Fishing castes worked the rivers and jhils with nets and paid dues to the local holder of fishing rights, a steady small revenue.",
