    title: "Wells, wheat and buffaloes in Ranjit Singh's Punjab",
    text: "In the Punjab of the misls and of Maharaja Ranjit Singh, the land between the rivers was farmed mainly by Jat peasant lineages, Sikh, Hindu and Muslim, who grew wheat, barley and gram in winter and maize, millets, cotton and sugarcane in summer. Away from the rivers, fields depended on brick-lined wells worked by the Persian wheel, a pair of bullocks turning a chain of pots from dawn to dusk. The state took its share by batai, dividing the heaped grain on the threshing floor, or by kankut, estimating the standing crop. Buffaloes gave the milk, butter and ghee that made the Punjabi diet famously rich.",
