    title: "The bazaars of Lahore and Amritsar",
    text: "Under Ranjit Singh, Amritsar grew into the commercial capital of Punjab, its katras (market quarters) dealing in grain, shawls, salt, horses and cloth, while Lahore held the court and its old walled bazaars around the Delhi and Shah Alami gates. The Nanakshahi rupee, struck at Amritsar and Lahore, was the currency. Khatri and Arora traders ran the shops and credit; caravans of camels and bullocks brought goods over the passes from Afghanistan and Kashmir and down from the hills, and Powindah caravans came down from Afghanistan every winter. Bargaining in the bazaar was ritualised, sometimes done silently with fingers under a cloth.",
