    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Ryotwari, zamindari and the cash crop",
    text: "Under British rule how a cultivator held his field depended on the settlement made there: in Bengal the Permanent Settlement of 1793 made zamindars owners and peasants their tenants; in Bombay and Madras the ryotwari system taxed each cultivator directly on a surveyed field, in cash, whatever the harvest. Cash payment pushed farmers toward crops that sold: cotton in the Deccan, jute in Bengal, wheat in Punjab, groundnut and sugarcane elsewhere, often grown on advances from traders. Village tools changed little: the wooden plough, the bullock, the sickle and the Persian wheel. Railways carried crops to ports, and prices now rose and fell with distant markets.",
