    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The Forest Department closes the jungle",
    text: "The Indian Forest Act of 1878 turned vast forests into reserves managed for timber, railway sleepers and revenue. Villagers and forest peoples who had grazed cattle, cut wood, gathered leaves and fruit, and cleared shifting plots there now needed permits, paid fees or became trespassers, and fines and forest guards became part of village life; resistance ran through revolts from the Rampa rebellion of 1879 to the forest satyagrahas of the 1920s and 1930s. Pastoralists lost grazing, and the Criminal Tribes Act of 1871 branded many wandering communities. Hunting passed to sportsmen with licences, and bounties were paid for killing tigers and wolves.",
