    text: "The colonial cities built covered markets: Crawford Market in Bombay (1869), with its reliefs designed by John Lockwood Kipling, and the Sir Stuart Hogg Market, the New Market, in Calcutta (1874), where British households bought meat and imported goods. Around them, the older bazaars of Burrabazar and Bhuleshwar continued, run by Marwari, Gujarati and Bohra traders dealing in cloth, grain, spices and hardware, with credit on hundi and account books. Street hawkers sold vegetables, snacks and sweets. Prices were in rupees, annas and pice; fixed prices were a novelty of European shops, and bargaining remained the rule everywhere else.",
