    text: "Colonial cities ran on carried loads. In Bombay, hamals carried bales at the docks and in the cotton market, and from the 1890s the dabbawalas carried home-cooked lunches in tiffin boxes from suburbs to offices by train and handcart. Hand-pulled rickshaws came to Calcutta around 1900, pulled mostly by migrants from Bihar; bullock carts, horse-drawn gharries and the horse tram served until electric trams arrived in 1902 in Calcutta and 1907 in Bombay. Porters met every train at the stations, and coolies of the docks and railway yards were hired by the day through a jobber or mukadam.",
