    text: "In Bombay most working families lived in chawls, three- or four-storey tenements of single rooms along a shared verandah, with a common tap and latrines at the end of the corridor; a family of six might cook, eat and sleep in a room ten feet square. In Calcutta the bustees were clusters of thatched or tiled huts around a tank. Women queued at the tap before dawn, cooked on coal or kerosene stoves, ground spices on a stone, washed clothes and packed the men's tiffin. Many took in piecework, rolled bidis or went out as domestic servants to add to the family's wages.",
