    text: "Malcolm Darling's study of 1925, The Punjab Peasant in Prosperity and Debt, found most cultivators owing money to the village bania or sahukar, who kept the bahi-khata ledger in a script the borrower could not read, compounding interest at twenty-five percent or more. Loans went to seed, cattle, rent, weddings and funerals. Under British law land became saleable and courts enforced bonds, so debt led to land passing to moneylenders, which prompted the Punjab Land Alienation Act of 1900. In Bengal the mahajan played the same role for jute growers, and co-operative credit societies from 1904 offered a slow alternative.",
