    text: "Classical Hindu law called the wife sahadharmini, partner in dharma: a man could not perform the household rites alone, and the couple sat together at sacrifices and pujas. The Kamasutra's book on wives describes a capable wife running the household economy, keeping accounts, buying stores in season, managing servants, tending the garden and the cattle, and saving against hard times. Later Tamil and Bhakti poetry and Muslim custom each had their own ideal of a married pair. A shared future meant sons to continue the line, land or a craft to pass on, and a household kept together; the ideal wife's devotion was praised, but real couples bargained and planned together.",
